    "body": "Image created with AI\nBy Charlotte Goldstone\n19 November 2026\nA strike could disrupt cargo operations at the ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach from Saturday, after the latest contract negotiations between employers and port security workers ended without agreement.\nThe Pacific Maritime Association (PMA), which represents shipping lines and terminal operators, warned yesterday that a walkout was a real possibility after talks with the International Longshore and Warehouse Union (ILWU) Local 26 broke down on Tuesday – each side blaming the other for the lack of progress.\nLocal 26 represents approximately 250 watchmen responsible for security duties, including checking credentials at terminal entrances and conducting patrols.\nAlthough they account for only around 2% of the ILWU workforce at the two ports, a strike could have significant consequences for cargo movements. According to the PMA, approximately 85% of Long Beach’s cargo and 33% of LA’s is handled at the terminals where Local 26 members work.\nPlus, the association warned that thousands of other ILWU workers, including longshore staff, marine clerks, and foremen, could refuse to cross picket lines, potentially bringing operations to a halt.\nYesterday, the PMA claimed the union had made little progress towards accepting its proposals and was unwilling to extend the current contract beyond 12:01 am on 21 November.\nIts offer included a one-time $3,200 bonus and wage increases that would bring average annual earnings for full-time watchmen to $198,000, alongside employer-funded healthcare and improved pension benefits.\nHowever, Local 26 president Luisa Graetz challenged the employers’ version of events. She told Seatrade Maritime News that the union had submitted a counterproposal, but employers had refused to respond. She also disputed the accuracy of the wage figures presented by the PMA.\nNegotiations have been ongoing for more than a year, with the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service assisting in efforts to reach an agreement.\nThe latest breakdown could come as a shock to many, after the two parties had previously agreed to several contract extensions to temporarily avert the threat of industrial action, most recently extending negotiations until 20 November.\nThe PMA also warned that a strike could have wide consequences for the US supply chain, affecting truck and rail movements, warehousing, and businesses dependent on imports and exports.\nThe association cited an economic analysis by maritime economist Dr John Martin, which estimated that the terminals concerned last year supported nearly 4.2 million jobs nationwide and generated $885bn in economic activity.\nDespite the threat of industrial action, negotiations are not over. Both parties are scheduled to return to the bargaining table tomorrow, leaving open the possibility of a last-minute agreement or further extension.",
